// @run
// @log 3
// GRADUATED 2026-09-01 by D1021's fix — this file used to pin
// `@emit-error function-value call arity has no interned signature`, and the program now
// runs and prints the value. The refusal was the same root: `L | null` as a closure RESULT
// interned no `$fnsig`, because rendering `L`'s self-reference through the cycle guard
// answered "" and the result kind could not be named. Rendering a revisit as the ALIAS NAME
// gives the signature a result to intern.
//
// Kept as a fixture rather than deleted: the shape below is still the one place
// `structIndexOfExpr`'s arena rung is wider than the name rung it fronts, and the account
// under this header is what says why that matters.
// THE RECURSIVE-INNER NULLABLE, pinned because it is the one shape where
// `structIndexOfExpr`'s arena rung is WIDER than the name rung it fronts.
//
// `nodeNulStructInnerName` needs a `TyNullable` whose inner is a `TyObj` AND a non-empty
// render. `tyToEmitName`'s cycle guard is an ancestor stack, so rendering `L` recurses through
// the `n` field, hits itself, and collapses the whole render to "". The arena rung needs only the
// first two conditions — and `repCanonId` handles the cycle with a de Bruijn back-edge rather
// than by declining — so it answers a row where the name rung answered nothing.
//
// BOTH STILL REJECT, which is why this is an `@emit-error` and not a `@run`: the shape has no
// interned closure signature either way. What the arena rung changes is WHICH reject — before it,
// `ref valtype with no interned shape`; after it, the arity message below. Pinning the message is
// what makes the widening visible if it ever stops being inert, because `structIndexOfExpr`
// returning >= 0 where it returned -1 is NOT inert in general: the Member arm's `msi >= 0` leg
// short-circuits a narrowed-variant fallback.
//
// A reviewer found this by reading the two predicates' preconditions side by side; no sweep
// contains it, and the corpus A/B for the change that introduced it is 0 of 2,032.
type L = { v: i32, n: L | null }

function mk(): (i32) => L | null {
  return (x) => if x > 0 { { v: x, n: null } } else { null }
}

const g = mk()
const r = g(3)
if r != null { print(r.v) }
